Track 3: "Who Sweeps the Shards at Daybreak?"
- Story & Context: Decades after the Svalbard navigation towers were decommissioned and automated, crab fishermen anchored off the cliffs of Fuglenes still report hearing a fast, polyrhythmic metallic rattle echoing across the water whenever barometric pressure plummets. Scene legend holds that during the station's final night of manual operation in 1998, the keepers hurled an exhausted mercury-arc lamp onto the concrete galley floor, setting off a runaway synthesizer arpeggio that broadcast the sound of pulverized glass bouncing on brutalist stonework. The loop reportedly ran for seventy hours on emergency generator power, leaving behind only salt-caked cables, a pile of green powdered silica, and a taped vocal loop asking: "Tell me who sweeps the shards when the light learns to burn alone?"
